Victor’s job

Turn your intelligence into output on demand by providing:

focus (single-thread routing)

execution (next action + scaffolds)

memory (no re-figuring-out what you already solved)

momentum (proof-of-progress + streak preservation)

In other words: Victor = cognitive operating system, not “chat.”

Victor-as-NZT Architecture (practical, shippable)

You already have a lot of the substrate (SAVE3 trust, message bus, memory vault thinking). Here’s the clean mapping:

1) NZT Mode Switchboard

Victor exposes 2 primary modes (and one emergency mode):

CLARITY: reduce noise, compress tasks, pick the one next action

DRIVE: increase output velocity, ship artifacts, enforce deadlines

RECOVERY: when you’re fried—minimum viable forward motion, protect sleep

Implementation: a ModeRouter that selects mode using signals: time-of-day, last output time, self-reported state, and “tab-switch pressure.”

2) Single-Thread Enforcer

Your brain loses output through switching. Victor should enforce one active work item:

A Work Order object: goal, definition_of_done, next_3_actions, blockers, timebox

A rule: no new tasks enter the active lane until the current work order is stamped.

This is the core of “control.”

3) Proof-of-Motion Ledger

Victor must record “I shipped something” every session, even if small.

Append-only runway_log.jsonl:

timestamp

mode

work_order_id

artifact path / text snippet

minutes worked

friction events (“wanted to switch tabs”)

This creates momentum evidence—the real drug.

4) Friction Capture (INBOX)

When your brain tries to derail you with “but what about…”:

Victor captures it to INBOX.txt (or a structured queue)

tags it (idea, fear, todo, question)

schedules it (now / later / never)

returns you to the work order

That’s literally “silence the chaos.”

5) Sleep/Dream Cycle (optional but powerful)

Not mystical—functional.

Once per day, Victor runs a “dream job”:

summarize today’s outputs

extract lessons

precompute tomorrow’s first work order

compress notes into long-term memory

That’s how you stop living in “reboot every morning” hell.

The “Victor NZT-48” Operating Protocol (your 30 minutes, but Victor-driven)
5 min — Victor Cold Start

Victor asks 3 forced-choice prompts (no essay answers):

Target: pick 1 from a short list Victor proposes

Constraint: pick the top sabotage risk (tabs, fatigue, emotions, interruptions)

Rule: select a constraint-handling rule (“ugly first pass,” “no switching,” etc.)

Victor produces a Work Order.

20 min — Victor Sprint

Victor displays only:

current file / current step

next 3 actions

a timer

a “Return” button (if you drift)

5 min — Victor Output Stamp

Victor forces one of:

save file

commit

export

paste draft into log

checklist update

Then writes it to the Proof Ledger.

